{"maxId":79,"entries":{"chapter-57-study-cognitive-changes-in-normal-and-pathologic-aging|Chapter 57 Study: Cognitive Changes in Normal and Pathologic Aging":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Crystallized (verbal) intelligence stays relatively intact with age; fluid intelligence (flexible reasoning/problem-solving) declines; reduced speed of information processing has been theorized to account for many of these changes (p853)":["u3"],"copy|Sustained attention (vigilance) does not change significantly with age; divided attention may decline, results mixed (p854)":["u4"],"copy|\"Real-world\" executive functions (planning, executing multi-step tasks) are little affected by normal aging even though performance on novel executive tasks (Wisconsin Card Sort, Stroop, Go-No-Go) declines slightly (p854)":["u5"],"copy|Remote memory, procedural memory, semantic memory stay intact; learning/recall of new information is what declines — slower learning curve, lower total learned (p854-855)":["u6"],"copy|Language: comprehension, vocabulary, and syntax are preserved; spontaneous word finding (\"tip-of-the-tongue\") and semantic fluency (\"name animals\") decline, while phonemic fluency (\"words starting with F\") stays stable (p856)":["u7"],"copy|Visuospatial: speed slows, but mental rotation and complex copy tasks can still worsen even when speed is controlled for (p856)":["u8"],"copy|Prevalence of medical conditions associated with cognitive impairment among US adults aged 65+ (Figure 57-1, p856): hypertension 70%, chronic kidney disease 38%, diabetes 27%, thyroid disease 25%, coronary artery disease 17%, sleep apnea 14%, depression 10-15%, COPD 10%":["u9"],"copy|Postoperative cognitive decline (POCD) after cardiac surgery: seen in 30-70% of patients at hospital discharge; still present in 15-25% at 12-month follow-up (p859)":["u10"],"copy|T2DM affects 20-30% of US adults >65; complex attention and verbal memory encoding are the main deficits (p859-860)":["u11"],"copy|CKD cognitive impairment prevalence 10-40%, depending on disease severity/duration (p860)":["u12"],"copy|High-dose vitamin E (2000 IU/day) reduced functional decline in mild-to-moderate AD in a large trial, though evidence for prevention is weak overall (p860-861)":["u13"],"copy|Depression prevalence in older adults: 11-30% (p861)":["u14"],"copy|Table 57-2's per-condition grid flags AD, VaD, and PD risk specifically for coronary artery disease, T2DM, and CKD; hypertension, cardiac surgery, COPD, and sleep apnea are flagged for AD and VaD only (no PD); nutritional deficiency for AD and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome; thyroid disease for AD alone (Table 57-2, p857-858)":["u15"],"copy|Per the table, several of these cognitive effects can improve with treatment (only limited evidence for cardiac rehab; improvement often during year 1 post-surgery; antihypertensives/antidiabetics, dialysis/transplant, O2 therapy, CPAP, thyroid replacement) — but B12/vitamin D deficits may persist despite supplementation, and some thyroid patients don't return to cognitive baseline (Table 57-2, p857-858)":["u16"],"copy|Of the major dementia types compared, AD and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome are the only ones with impaired recognition (not just recall) on memory testing — the table marks both \"Recognition intact?\" N; PD/PDD, DLB, and VaD are marked variable; DLB is distinguished by significant fluctuations in attention plus early hallucinations/delusions; vascular dementia: onset insidious or acute, progression stepwise or gradual, with executive deficits significantly more impaired than in AD and relative to verbal memory performance":["u17"],"copy|Behavioral variant FTLD: early disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy/sympathy, hyperorality, with recent memory relatively preserved; PSP: vertical gaze palsy + postural instability; CBS: asymmetric motor signs + alien limb; NPH: wide-based gait, urinary incontinence, confusional state":["u18"],"copy|NIA/AA criteria: (1) cognitive concern, (2) objective impairment in ≥1 domain, (3) preserved functional independence, (4) no dementia (p868-869)":["u19"],"copy|80% of those diagnosed with MCI progress to AD within 5-8 years; conversion rate ~10-15%/year vs 1-2%/year in the general population (p869)":["u20"],"copy|15-40% of MCI patients later \"revert\" to normal on testing — more likely if community-based (not clinic-based), biomarker-negative, younger, fewer comorbidities, more education (p869)":["u21"],"copy|Underlying AD neuropathology (β-amyloid, neurofibrillary tangles) typically begins ~20 years or more before clinical symptom onset (p862)":["u22"],"copy|NIA/AA dementia criteria require impairment interfering with daily function across ≥2 cognitive domains (p862)":["u23"],"copy|Memory (anterograde amnesia/rapid forgetting) is the earliest and most common first symptom; semantic (\"category\") fluency is impaired disproportionately to phonemic fluency (p867-868)":["u24"],"copy|Onset typically late 50s to early 60s — the 2nd most common dementia in patients under 65 (p873)":["u25"],"copy|30-40% of FTLD cases are linked to genetic mutations (GRN, MAPT) (p873)":["u26"],"copy|Behavioral variant FTLD (bvFTD) accounts for 50-60% of all FTLD diagnoses (p873)":["u27"],"copy|Up to 75% of pathologically confirmed FTLD patients also meet clinical criteria for probable AD (p873)":["u28"],"copy|Upwards of 80% of PD patients who live ≥20 years are expected to develop PDD (p870)":["u29"],"copy|PD-MCI prevalence ~30-40%; 10-30% of newly diagnosed PD patients already show cognitive impairment (p870)":["u30"],"copy|DLB clue: visual hallucinations with MMSE >20 are highly suggestive of DLB rather than AD (p871)":["u31"],"copy|PD→PDD requires dementia onset >1 year after established motor symptoms; if dementia precedes or comes within 1 year of motor onset, it's classified DLB (p870)":["u32"],"copy|Co-occurrence of AD + VaD pathology: 20-40% of dementia patients (p872)":["u33"],"copy|Sporadic prion disease (CJD): typically diagnosed 60s, age range 40-80 (p876)":["u34"],"copy|Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) makes up ~6% of dementia cases; classic triad = gait/balance disturbance, urinary incontinence, cognitive deficits; unlike most other dementias, cognitive symptoms often present later in the course (p876)":["u35"],"copy|NPH shunt outcomes: at 6 months, 83% improved gait and 46% improved memory; at 5 years, only 39% of surviving shunt cases remained gait-improved and <10% still showed cognitive improvement (p876)":["u36"],"copy|CDC reported that over half of people living with diagnosed HIV in the US were aged 50 or older at the end of 2021. Hazzard cites an older 10% figure and reports 2–3% progression to HIV-associated dementia with combination ART and 50–60% prevalence of milder cognitive deficits in the cohorts it reviews (p876).":["u37"],"copy|Neurosyphilis dementia classically emerges 5-25 years after initial infection (p876)":["u38"],"recall|Recall":["u39"],"copy|CHAPTER 57s / HAZZARD":["u40"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 57; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u41"],"copy|Normal aging — what declines vs what's preserved":["u42"],"copy|Common medical conditions and cognition (Table 57-2, Figure 57-1)":["u43"],"copy|Differentiating dementia types (Table 57-3, p863-866)":["u44"],"copy|MCI due to AD":["u45"],"copy|AD dementia":["u46"],"copy|FTLD":["u47"],"copy|PD / PDD / DLB":["u48"],"copy|Vascular cognitive impairment (VCI)":["u49"],"copy|Other reversible/less common dementias":["u50"],"copy|1.":["u51"],"copy|In normal aging, which type of intelligence is relatively preserved and which declines?":["u52"],"copy|Crystallized (verbal) intelligence is relatively preserved; fluid intelligence (flexible reasoning/problem-solving) declines (p853).":["u53"],"copy|2.":["u54"],"copy|Which aspect of attention holds up well with normal aging, and which may decline?":["u55"],"copy|Sustained attention (vigilance) is preserved; divided attention (attending to more than one thing at once) may decline, though results are mixed (p854).":["u56"],"copy|4.":["u57"],"copy|In normal aging, which verbal fluency type declines (semantic or phonemic), and which stays stable?":["u58"],"copy|Semantic fluency (\"name all the animals you can\") declines; phonemic fluency (\"words starting with F\") generally remains stable (p856).":["u59"],"copy|6.":["u60"],"copy|What proportion of patients show postoperative cognitive decline (POCD) after cardiac surgery at hospital discharge, and at 12 months?":["u61"],"copy|30-70% at discharge; still present in 15-25% at 12-month follow-up (p859).":["u62"],"copy|8.":["u63"],"copy|What fraction of patients diagnosed with MCI progress to AD, and over what time frame?":["u64"],"copy|About 80% progress to AD within 5 to 8 years, converting at roughly 10-15%/year vs 1-2%/year in the general population (p869).":["u65"],"copy|14.":["u66"],"copy|What percentage of dementia cases does normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) make up, and what are its shunt-surgery gait/memory outcomes at 6 months vs 5 years?":["u67"],"copy|NPH makes up about 6% of dementia cases.":["u68"],"copy|At 6 months post-shunt, 83% improve in gait and 46% improve in memory; of surviving shunt cases at 5 years, only 39% remain gait-improved and fewer than 10% still show cognitive improvement (p876).":["u69"],"copy|15.":["u70"],"copy|What proportion of people living with diagnosed HIV in the US were aged 50 or older in CDC's 2021 data, and how does the chapter distinguish dementia from milder cognitive deficits?":["u71"],"copy|Over half were 50 or older at the end of 2021 in CDC surveillance.":["u72"],"copy|The chapter's treated cohorts had an estimated 2–3% progression to HIV-associated dementia, while milder cognitive deficits were more frequent (50–60%, p876).":["u72"],"copy|Answer":["u73","u74","u75","u76","u77","u78","u79"],"normal-aging-what-declines-vs-what-39-s-preserved|Normal aging — what declines vs what's preserved":["u42"],"common-medical-conditions-and-cognition-table-57-2-figure-57-1|Common medical conditions and cognition (Table 57-2, Figure 57-1)":["u43"],"differentiating-dementia-types-table-57-3-p863-866|Differentiating dementia types (Table 57-3, p863-866)":["u44"],"mci-due-to-ad|MCI due to AD":["u45"],"ad-dementia|AD dementia":["u46"],"ftld|FTLD":["u47"],"pd-pdd-dlb|PD / PDD / DLB":["u48"],"vascular-cognitive-impairment-vci|Vascular cognitive impairment (VCI)":["u49"],"other-reversible-less-common-dementias|Other reversible/less common dementias":["u50"]}}
